{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural obligation is ownership: \"TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission\". An alternative that hands these to an external agent framework would violate it. The text names no library, signature, or type, so the roots only need to show that TBV's own code holds each of the four pieces.\n\n- **Backend integrations:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/providers.rs#impl[RigProvider]/fn[connect]` builds the Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama completion models from `BackendConfig`. It carries `{impl}` for this requirement.\n- **Conversation:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tools.rs#impl[ToolAgent<P, T>]#2/fn[run_with_domain_tools]` builds the opening `CompletionRequest`. That request holds the subject message, the node inputs and the prefix, which becomes the preamble. It then calls TBV's own `converse` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent.rs#fn[converse]`, passing the provider, the session, `result_schema` and `finish`. It carries `{impl}`.\n- **Tool dispatch:** `run_with_domain_tools` creates a `GraphSession` over `SessionTools` and the registered domain tools. `converse` uses it through the `Conversation` trait. Its `dispatch` is `tools.rs#impl[GraphSession<'_, '_, D>/Conversation]/fn[dispatch]`, which handles `read_node`, `outgoing_edges` and the other tools. A reviewer reaches this code from the marked root, so it needs no marker of its own.\n- **Structured submission:** `converse` takes the result schema and the `finish` callback, and `run_with_domain_tools` tells the model to finish by calling the submission tool. `converse` is reached from the marked root, so it needs no separate marker.\n\nEvery structural piece the text names is covered by the two supplied roots plus the code they reach. I found no gap.\n\nbehavioral: `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent` is an ownership statement. TBV owns four things: the model conversation, the supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission. The detailed obligations sit in refining requirements, which are not reassessed here: `tool_agent.isolation`, `submission*`, `fixed_tools`, the `*Config` requirements and `tool_agent.provider_failure`. The observable behaviour that remains, and the tests for it, are:\n\n- **Conversation, tool dispatch and submission (backend-independent).** These run through `ToolAgent` with scripted providers.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer]` asserts the system and user messages sent. It also asserts the `submit_result` schema and that the typed answer is returned, fingerprinted and stored.\n  - `#fn[application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation]` asserts that every request offers the graph tools, the application tool and `submit_result`. It also asserts that multi-turn tool results are delivered back in order and that paging works.\n  - `#fn[application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call]` asserts that application tools are dispatched and that each agent call gets fresh tool state.\n  - `#fn[agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces]` and `#fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` cover concurrent conversations. They assert that histories, tool usage and dependencies stay separate, and that each conversation reaches its own submission.\n- **Supplied backend integrations.**\n  - OpenAI: `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport]` runs independent calls against a local transport. It asserts the model, the output limit and the tool list including `submit_result` and its schema. It also asserts that the prompt reaches the model, and that the tool result comes back in the next request as a `function_call_output`.\n  - Ollama: `#fn[supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat]` uses a native chat transport. It asserts the endpoint, bearer authorization and options. It also asserts the tool schema and that the tool result is delivered on the second turn.\n  - Bedrock: `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/providers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history]` checks that tool calls and native history are normalised in order. `#fn[cache_checkpoints_are_requested_for_caching_models]` and `#fn[bedrock_native_option_types_are_checked_and_only_max_tokens_is_defaulted]` check the request-side behaviour. Bedrock has no end-to-end test against a local transport. Its AWS-backed transport would need real network services, and the Bedrock-specific behaviour in the conversation path is covered by these unit tests.\n\nEvery obligation I could derive is mapped to a test with real assertions, and I found no unacknowledged gap. Provider failures, submission validity and the other detailed behaviours are delegated to their own requirements.",
